=== the verdict this decides ===
   c = 1.4 : e/Gamma runs 0.971 0.131 0.220 0.060 0.061 0.017 0.002 0.021 0.017
             r(n) runs      8 34 52 134 242 528 1070 2216 4516
   c = 1.6 : e/Gamma runs 0.275 0.105 0.763 0.028 0.182 0.161
             r(n) runs      6 6 6 16 20 44

   If e/Gamma is O(1) and not falling, the limit lm/r -> Gamma has not begun at these k
   and NOTHING about its rate can be measured -- neither hypothesis is being tested.
   If it is falling, fit the rate and compare with log(2/c).

   c = 1.4 : fitted rate 0.1141 against log(2/c) = 0.3567 ; residual spread in log e = 3.20
   c = 1.6 : fitted rate 0.0262 against log(2/c) = 0.2231 ; residual spread in log e = 3.24

   A residual spread of order 1 in log e means the fitted rate carries an uncertainty of
   roughly spread/(k-range), and that number must be printed beside the rate or the
   agreement is being asserted rather than measured.
